# Milestone 04 — Extension: Predictive Ridge Tracking

Retain the previously defined frequency-constrained first-order DP as the reference implementation.

Add predictive tracking as a second stage, after the first-order reference is validated.

## Scientific motivation

A physical oscillatory mode is expected to have a locally continuous instantaneous frequency and, in many cases, a locally continuous chirp rate.

Use previous frequency estimates to predict future candidate positions rather than considering every frequency bin equally plausible.

The supplied frequency interval remains a hard constraint.

## 1. Predictor

For uniform time steps:

    f_pred[n+1] = f[n] + (f[n] - f[n-1])

For nonuniform time steps:

    slope = (f[n] - f[n-1]) / (t[n] - t[n-1])

    f_pred[n+1] = f[n] + slope*(t[n+1]-t[n])

Support constant-frequency prediction as the initial fallback before two ridge points are available.

An optional acceleration predictor may use three or more observations, but do not introduce it before testing the constant-velocity predictor.

## 2. Second-order cost

Implement a trajectory objective consisting of:

- Observation cost from WSST complex coefficient magnitude.
- First-order frequency-change penalty.
- Second-order prediction-error/curvature penalty.
- User-specified frequency-range restrictions.
- Optional maximum physical frequency-change rate.
- Optional maximum physical chirp-rate-change constraint.

For nonuniform time coordinates, define curvature using differences of physical frequency slopes rather than unscaled second differences.

Use explicit reference scales so all penalties are dimensionless.

## 3. Exact reference solver

Implement second-order DP with state consisting of two consecutive frequency indices.

For a candidate transition:

    (i, k) -> (k, j)

evaluate the exact accumulated objective.

Support frequency-constrained and banded transitions.

Backtrack to return the optimal complete trajectory.

Test against exhaustive enumeration on small frequency-time matrices.

Use deterministic tie-breaking.

## 4. Predictive pruning and acceleration

Optionally construct a local candidate window centered on:

    f_pred = f[k] + slope(i,k)*Δt

The window may be defined in Hz or in units of an explicitly modeled prediction uncertainty.

Distinguish:

- A hard physical constraint.
- A heuristic candidate restriction.
- A soft prediction-error penalty.

Do not claim global optimality for a pruned solver relative to an unconstrained objective unless the pruning is proven exact.

Consider beam search only if the exact banded second-order solver is computationally too expensive.

## 5. Startup

At the first sample, estimate the initial frequency within the supplied interval.

At the second sample, use the ordinary first-order transition cost.

From the third sample onward, apply the predictive second-order objective.

Do not commit permanently to a single starting frequency when running exact offline DP: retain competing initial states.

Allow optional initial frequency and initial slope priors.

## 6. Prediction uncertainty

Provide a configurable prediction bandwidth.

A fixed-Hz search window is sufficient initially.

Do not automatically shrink the prediction window merely because more samples have been processed.

Increasing certainty requires an explicit model of observation errors and process variability.

If a later Kalman-style estimator is introduced, distinguish its modeled covariance from empirical accuracy.

## 7. Robustness

Test:

- Constant frequency.
- Linear chirp.
- Quadratic chirp.
- Smooth nonlinear chirp.
- Abrupt slope change.
- Noisy frequency observations.
- Short gaps.
- Weak mode near a stronger mode.
- Incorrect starting frequency.
- Incorrect initial slope.
- Crossing modes.
- Nonuniform time steps.
- Narrow and wide prediction windows.

Compare first-order DP, second-order DP, and any approximate predictor on identical precomputed WSST matrices.

Measure ridge RMSE, coverage, identity switching, total objective cost, runtime, and allocations.

A predictive tracker must not be considered superior merely because its output looks smoother.

## 8. Acceptance

The extension passes when:

1. Second-order DP agrees with exhaustive enumeration.
2. A linear chirp incurs zero interior discrete curvature penalty on a uniform grid when represented exactly.
3. Predictive tracking improves or preserves recovery in the targeted smooth-chirp cases.
4. Rapid genuine changes are not silently smoothed away.
5. Frequency ranges are always enforced.
6. Computational cost and approximation behavior are documented.
7. The production implementation falls back to first-order DP when insufficient information is available or when predictive constraints are inappropriate.
